      "inputTokens": 143,
      "outputTokens": 1006,
      "costUsd": 0.0091255,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's the key idea. Turn the dial up, and the bell slides to the right, so four ninety ends up further out in the tail, and the tail gets smaller. At a mean of five hundred, the tail was zero point one zero five six. Fill two hundred bottles there, and in one simulated run, nineteen come up short. Keep turning, and at some point the tail is exactly one percent. In sigmas, every normal curve is the same curve. So a one percent tail always ends the same number of sigmas below the mean, whatever the mean is. That number is a zed score. So the job is to find the zed first, then the mean."
